  "details": "<p>This\u00a0<span class=\"highlightedSearchTerm\">archive</span>\u00a0is located in the\u00a0Northwest Palace.</p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"418\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan66_general_plan_Kalhu1-17_p144.png\" width=\"452\" /></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"574\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan69_northwest_palace_Kalhu6-11_p149.png\" width=\"435\" />\u00a0</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Most documents found in the Northwest Palace, built by the Assyrian king Ashurnarsipal II (883\u2013859 BC), predate the move of the new Assyrian capital to Dur-\u0160arrukin under Sargon II (721\u2013705 BC). A few tablets were found in Rooms ZT 30 and ZT 31, on the eastern side of the northernmost courtyard of the palace. The administrative texts found in this archive repository include lists of quantities of oil (delivered to the palace), which were presumably stored in the large jars that were also found inside both rooms.",
